Title: Blog about the case report: TAV-in-TAV-in-TAV after treated endocarditis: procedural strategy, imaging planning and 1-year outcome

## When Is TAVR-After-TAVR-After-TAVR Reasonable? Redo TAVI After Treated PVE

This case is one of those “you’ll see this more often” warnings: a patient in his late 70s with three prior transcatheter aortic valves (Lotus → Evolut → Acurate neo2) plus a documented episode of Enterococcus faecalis prosthetic valve endocarditis (PVE) underwent a CT-planned third TAVI with excellent 1‑year outcome. What makes it surprising — and instructive — is not only that a third valve worked, but that the team performed it safely after treated PVE, using imaging-driven strategy to manage coronary risk, commissural alignment and prosthesis‑patient mismatch concerns.

Why this matters now
- TAVI volumes are rising and patients are living longer. Repeat transcatheter interventions (and the thorny problem of “frame layering”) are becoming part of lifetime valve care.
- Guidelines still emphasize surgery for complicated PVE, but real-world patients are often poor surgical candidates. We need practical, evidence‑informed criteria and procedural strategies for when a transcatheter approach is reasonable after treated PVE.

How the team justified a third TAVI after Enterococcus PVE
This case demonstrates a deliberate, multidisciplinary pathway that can be generalized when considering redo TAVI after treated PVE. The decision pivots on three pillars: infection control, prohibitive surgical risk/anatomy, and CT/TEE-confirmed feasibility.

1) Rigorous demonstration of infection control before reintervention
Reintervening into a previously infected prosthetic valve is intuitively risky. Before any transcatheter plan, the team ensured there was no active infection:
- Completed recommended antimicrobial therapy for Enterococcus faecalis (full intravenous course with infectious disease guidance).
- Serial negative blood cultures prior to procedure.
- Detailed transesophageal echocardiography (TEE) showing no vegetations, no paravalvular abscess or dehiscence.
- Multislice CT (MSCT) with no signs suggestive of active infection and used for structural assessment.
- Clinical stability without ongoing fevers, leukocytosis or bacteraemia.
That constellation — clinical, microbiologic and imaging evidence of resolution — is essential before contemplating TAV‑in‑TAV (or TAV‑in‑TAV‑in‑TAV).

2) Surgical risk and individualized heart‑team decision
Escalation to repeat surgery is guideline‑preferred in many PVE scenarios, but not always realistic. In this patient:
- EuroSCORE II ~27% and multiple comorbidities (COPD, diabetes, prior GI bleeding, prior CABG/PCI history, pacemaker).
- Two prior transcatheter frames integrated into the root — surgical explant would likely require complex root/ascending aortic reconstruction.
These factors pushed the balance toward a less invasive option. The heart team (interventionalists, cardiac surgeons, imaging, ID, anesthesiology) explicitly reviewed the case and involved the patient/family in shared decision-making — a must.

3) CT-guided feasibility and procedure planning
Detailed MSCT was the cornerstone. For stacked valves you must quantify:
- Minimum lumen inside the prior frame (this case: 13.7 mm at Evolut).
- Valve‑to‑coronary distances, sinus dimensions, and predicted neo‑skirt height.
- Interaction of prior leaflets/frames and ability to achieve a supra‑annular valve position without occluding coronaries.
Preprocedural CT allowed the team to:
- Select a valve with favorable supra‑annular leaflet position and lower frame height (Acurate neo2).
- Plan high implantation to pin prior leaflets outward and minimise a neo‑skirt crossing the coronaries.
- Decide coronary protection was not needed based on measured distances and modelling.
- Target commissural alignment to preserve future coronary access.

Technical pearls from the procedure
- Use CT-based 3D modelling (even if informal) to visualise how a new valve will sit relative to prior frames and coronaries.
- Favor valves with supra‑annular leaflets and lower frame height when the landing zone is small; commissural alignment matters for future coronary access.
- High implant strategy can mitigate creation of a sub‑coronary neo‑skirt and help preserve effective orifice area by pinning prior leaflets open.
- Pre‑dilation and high‑pressure post‑dilation may partially expand prior self‑expanding frames and improve effective orifice area — consider this carefully (and avoid uncontrolled fracture attempts unless planned).
- Keep coronary protection plans ready; bail‑out options (wiring coronaries, balloon/stent) should be available if angiography during inflation suggests flow compromise.
- Meticulous intraprocedural imaging (aortography, TTE/TEE when useful) to confirm no obstruction and to assess gradients.

Outcome and realistic expectations
- Immediate post‑implant haemodynamics may be deceptive: immediate mean gradient was high (67 mm Hg) but dropped to 27 mm Hg at 1 month and remained 26 mm Hg at 1 year with AVA ≈1.1 cm² and EF 65%.
- Residual prosthesis‑patient mismatch (PPM) is likely in triple-stented anatomy; the goal is acceptable clinical status and avoidance of heart failure or recurrent infection.
- Durability data are sparse; the heart team and patient must accept that future options (surgical explant or complex catheter maneuvers like BASILICA in stacked frames) may be limited or high risk.

Where guidelines and pragmatism meet
Current guidelines continue to favor surgery for many PVE cases, especially with complications or uncontrolled infection. This case does not contradict that; rather it shows how, when surgical risk is prohibitive and infection is convincingly eradicated, a carefully planned, heart‑team‑led transcatheter approach can be reasonable. Key is documenting eradication of infection and ensuring anatomic feasibility via CT/TEE.

Clinical take‑home messages
- Before reintervening after PVE, require microbiologic (serial negative blood cultures), clinical (no fever, normal inflammatory markers) and imaging (TEE and CT without vegetations/abscess) evidence of infection resolution.
- Engage a formal heart team and shared decision-making — surgical risk, anatomy and patient values must drive the plan.
- Use MSCT to evaluate valve‑to‑coronary distances, neo‑skirt risk, and minimal lumen inside prior frames; this imaging guides valve choice, implantation depth and need for coronary protection.
- Favor supra‑annular, low‑frame prostheses and commissural alignment strategies to preserve coronary access and optimise effective orifice area in stacked valves.
- Expect some degree of PPM; focus on clinical improvement and close follow‑up. Have bailout plans for coronary compromise ready.
- Documented success at 1 year is encouraging but long‑term durability of TAV‑in‑TAV‑in‑TAV remains uncertain — counsel patients accordingly.

Bottom line: A third TAVI after treated PVE is not routine, but it can be a reasonable, patient‑centred option when infection control is well documented, surgical risk is prohibitive, CT/TEE show anatomic feasibility, and a heart team commits to a deliberate, imaging‑guided procedural strategy.



# Triple Frames, Open Coronaries: CT-First Strategy to Prevent Coronary Obstruction

What grabs you about this case? A man in his late 70s with two prior TAVIs (Lotus 2014 → Evolut 2022), treated Enterococcus faecalis prosthetic valve endocarditis, and then a third transcatheter valve (Acurate neo2, 2024) implanted into the double-stented anatomy. No coronary protection was used. With CT-led planning, high implantation, commissural alignment and targeted device choice, the team avoided coronary obstruction and delivered durable 1‑year relief (mean gradient ~26 mm Hg, AVA 1.1 cm², EF 65%). Triple-frame TAVI is uncommon, and the PVE history makes the case feel even riskier — yet it teaches a practical, reproducible imaging-first approach.

## Why CT-first matters in valve-in-valve-in-valve cases
Each added stent frame changes the landing zone geometry, creates potential neo-skirts and increases the mesh burden that coronary catheters must traverse later. Plain fluoroscopy or transthoracic echo won’t reliably quantify the spatial relationships that matter most for coronary safety:

- Coronary ostial height alone is insufficient once you have stacked frames — you must measure valve-to-coronary (VTC) distance and model the anticipated neo-skirt.
- Sinus of Valsalva dimensions determine whether leaflets or skirt material can be pushed aside or will create a funnel that blocks flow.
- CT lets you see how the previous valve frames sit relative to the sinuses, commissures and coronary ostia and estimate whether a new supra-annular leaf will create a covered column.

In this case MSCT showed a minimum lumen diameter of 13.7 mm inside the Evolut frame and enough clearance to proceed without protection after careful modelling. That CT guided valve selection (Acurate neo2), implantation depth and the decision against prophylactic coronary wiring or chimney stenting.

## The practical CT playbook (stepwise)
1. Start with high-resolution MSCT with thin-slice, contrast-enhanced gated acquisition through the aortic root and ascending aorta.
2. Measure:
   - Coronary ostial heights from native annulus (but interpret cautiously with prior frames).
   - Sinus of Valsalva diameters and height.
   - Valve-to-coronary (VTC) distance — distance from anticipated neo‑skirt plane to coronary ostium.
   - Internal lumen and frame geometry of prior THVs (minimum lumen diameter, frame orientation).
3. Model the anticipated neo-skirt: plan where the new valve’s skirt/leaflets will sit relative to coronary ostia — if a supra-annular valve’s leaflets will align above the coronary ostia, risk is lower.
4. Assess commissural orientation of prior implants and feasibility of achieving commissural alignment with the planned device.
5. Decide on coronary protection strategy only after the above: if VTC is short, sinuses small, or model predicts skirt-coronary overlap → prepare for protection (wiring ± undeployed stent or BASILICA if applicable).

## How the imaging findings drive strategy
- Small VTC (<4 mm in many expert centers) + shallow sinuses → high risk of coronary obstruction; plan for protection or alternative strategy.
- Adequate VTC and roomy sinuses → consider high implantation, supra-annular device with low frame height to minimize neo-skirt and preserve access.
- If prior THV leaflets are bulky/fused (degeneration or post-endocarditis restriction), high implantation that tethers those leaflets against the frame can prevent inward leaflet folding that would further reduce the orifice area.

In our case the team had clear CT evidence that a high implant of a short-framed, supra-annular Acurate neo2 would place the new leaflets in a position that minimized the neo-skirt under the coronaries and preserved access — so they proceeded without coronary protection.

## Device selection: prioritize what preserves coronaries and EOAi
When you're dealing with stacked frames, think performance AND future access:
- Supra-annular valves (Acurate neo2, some self-expanding systems) often give better EOA in small effective annuli and can sit higher, reducing skirt coverage of coronaries.
- Low/short frames reduce mesh burden across the sinuses and facilitate later coronary catheterization.
- Devices that permit commissural alignment are preferred — if you can align new commissures to avoid overlapping existing posts, you decrease the chance of ostial coverage and simplify future PCI.

The Acurate neo2 was chosen here for supra‑annular leaflets, shorter frame height and commissural alignment capability — all helpful in triple-frame anatomy.

## Implant technique pearls
- Aim for high implantation when CT suggests that keeping the new leaflet plane at or above prior leaflet tips will prevent inward leaflet folding and minimize neo-skirt at the coronary level.
- Use CT and fluoroscopic landmarks for commissural alignment during deployment to keep posts away from coronaries.
- Balloon predilation can help assess coronary flow dynamically (as done here) — run an aortogram during balloon inflation to confirm no compromise before valve release.
- Post-dilation can expand prior frames (partial mechanical expansion) and improve gradients — but be mindful of risks (annular injury, embolization). In this case, post-dilation improved effective orifice and reduced gradients.
- Coronary protection should remain an on‑table bailout plan even if not used prophylactically: have guidewires and undeployed stents ready if angiography suggests slowing flow.

## When prior endocarditis changes the equation
A history of treated PVE raises infection‑related concerns (ongoing bacteremia, paravalvular extension) — but once infection is proven resolved clinically, microbiologically and on TEE/CT, decision-making reverts to anatomy and surgical risk. This patient had completed targeted antibiotics, had no active infection on TEE/MSCT, and was prohibitive for redo surgery — so CT-based TAV‑in‑TAV‑in‑TAV was reasonable and successful.

## Risks and the limits of strategy
- Even with ideal CT planning, triple frames increase the chance of reduced EOA and clinically significant patient-prosthesis mismatch (PPM). Expect possible high immediate gradients, which may improve after post-dilation or remodeling — as occurred here (initial mean gradient 67 mm Hg → 27 mm Hg at 1 month).
- Future coronary access may still be technically challenging despite commissural alignment; plan accordingly, and keep meticulous documentation of commissural orientation for downstream operators.
- Surgical explant after multiple TAVIs is technically demanding and high-risk — discuss limited future options with the patient and family during consent.

## The bottom line — clinical takeaways
- Detailed CT assessment (coronary height, sinus dimensions, VTC, predicted neo‑skirt and frame interaction) is indispensable before undertaking TAV‑in‑TAV‑in‑TAV.
- Device selection should prioritize supra‑annular leaflet position, shorter frame height and commissural alignment capacity when preserving coronary flow and future access is a priority.
- High implantation, guided by CT modelling, can tether old leaflets and minimize neo‑skirt formation beneath coronary ostia — reducing obstruction risk without routine prophylactic coronary protection.
- Have coronary protection as an on‑table bailout; use it prophylactically when CT predicts short VTC, small sinuses or likely skirt‑coronary overlap.
- Even in patients with prior treated PVE, if infection is eradicated and CT/TEE exclude active disease, a CT-first transcatheter strategy can be an acceptable alternative to high‑risk surgery — but requires multidisciplinary discussion and informed consent about limited future options.

Key takeaways:
- CT-first: always quantify VTC, sinus dimensions and frame anatomy before repeat TAVI.
- Choose a supra‑annular, low/short‑frame valve when coronary preservation and EOA matter.
- Aim for high implantation and commissural alignment to minimize neo-skirt and preserve access.
- Reserve routine coronary protection for high-risk CT features; keep it ready as a bailout.
- In treated PVE with no active infection, CT-guided TAV‑in‑TAV‑in‑TAV can be feasible — but plan lifetime management up front.

This patient’s result — symptom-free at 1 year with sustained haemodynamic improvement — shows the payoff of meticulous imaging, device choice and strategy in the triple-frame setting.



# The Scary Post-TAVR Gradient That Improved: PPM, Underexpansion, or Flow?

What makes this case catchy is embarrassingly familiar: immediate post-implant echo shows a terrifying mean gradient (67 mm Hg) after a technically successful TAV-in-TAV-in-TAV, and then—surprise—gradients settle to a reasonable 26–27 mm Hg by 1 month with clinical improvement. That trajectory forces you to ask: was this true severe prosthesis–patient mismatch (PPM) that somehow resolved, mechanical underexpansion that self-corrected, a temporary low-flow/high-gradient physiology, or simply an echo/flow artefact? The answer matters because our management options (watchful waiting, balloon post‑dilation, acceptable reintervention) carry very different risks in a heavily stented root.

This post walks through a practical, CT- and echo‑driven approach to a high immediate post‑TAVI gradient—how to think through the mechanisms, what measurements help you decide, and pragmatic thresholds for observation, further dilation, or reintervention anchored to VARC-3 endpoints.

## Why this pattern happens (mechanisms)
Several non‑mutually exclusive mechanisms can produce an alarming immediate high gradient that improves over days–weeks:

- Mechanical underexpansion: incomplete radial expansion of the new THV due to a rigid prior frame(s). Post‑dilatation or gradual radial creep of a self‑expanding frame can improve orifice area over time.
- Low‑flow/high‑gradient physiology: immediately post‑implant the LV may be stunned, pacing may transiently alter hemodynamics, or low stroke volume may create artificially high gradients despite small effective orifice area. As LV output recovers (or volumes change), gradients fall.
- Pressure recovery / Doppler misinterpretation: in small aortic roots or overlapping frames, Doppler may overestimate gradients; careful use of continuity equation and DVI mitigates this.
- True severe PPM: the implanted valve’s effective orifice area (EOA) is too small for patient body size and will remain high gradient chronically.
- Valve leaflet restriction from residual tissue, thrombus, or incomplete expansion—may improve with dilation or time if partial expansion occurs.

In the presented case the immediate mean gradient was 67 mm Hg after an Acurate neo2 was implanted inside an Evolut inside a Lotus. Post‑dilation was performed and coronary flow remained preserved. By 1 month the mean gradient had fallen to 27 mm Hg with AVA 1.1 cm² and clinical recovery—favouring partially reversible underexpansion/flow effects rather than fixed catastrophic PPM.

## Key measurements to help discriminate causes
Don’t rely on gradient alone. Use multiple, concordant datapoints:

- Indexed AVA (AVA/BSA): best metric for PPM. Severe PPM typically indexed AVA ≤0.65 cm²/m² (sometimes ≤0.6 depending on source); moderate 0.65–0.85 cm²/m². In the case the indexed AVA immediately post‑implant was 0.4 cm²/m² (severe), later improved to an AVA 1.1 cm² (indexed ~0.44–0.5 depending on BSA)—still small but much better hemodynamics.
- Doppler Velocity Index (DVI = LVOT VTI / aortic VTI): less flow‑dependent than calculated AVA. DVI <0.25 suggests severe stenosis; 0.25–0.50 is moderate‑severe zone. Here DVI was 0.24 immediately (severe), but clinical course matters.
- Stroke volume index / flow status: low stroke volume index (<35 mL/m²) can create low‑flow, high‑gradient states. Document whether stroke volume/EF are depressed immediately post‑implant and whether they recover.
- Valve morphology on TTE/TEE: leaflet motion, presence of paravalvular leak, gradients across frame layers.
- CT imaging: helps identify underexpansion, frame interaction, valve‑to‑coronary distances and whether there is room for further dilation or potential for coronary compromise with post‑dilation.
- Temporal trend: repeat TTE at 24–72 hours and at 1 month. Rapid improvement points to reversible causes.

## Practical algorithm: what to do in the cath lab, immediate post‑implant, and early follow‑up

1. Immediate intra‑procedure (after deployment)
   - Confirm correct position and absence of significant paravalvular leak on angiography and echo.
   - If gradient is high, first exclude technical issues: malposition, wire entrapment, severe regurgitation, or catastrophic coronary obstruction.
   - Check LV function and hemodynamics: measure transvalvular gradient invasively if uncertain.
   - If underexpansion is suspected and anatomy allows (CT review supports safety), consider cautious balloon post‑dilatation. In heavily layered frames (TAV-in-TAV-in-TAV) anticipate higher radial resistance and risk to coronary flow—plan for coronary protection if repeat dilation is aggressive.
   - If borderline but anatomy risky for further dilation (or if coronary risk unacceptable), consider conservative management with close monitoring.

2. Immediately post‑procedure (first 24–72 hours)
   - Obtain transthoracic echo with DVI, AVA (continuity), peak/mean gradients, stroke volume index, and evaluation of leaflet motion.
   - If DVI <0.25 and indexed AVA ≤0.6 with preserved stroke volume and symptoms, strongly consider reintervention/optimization if safe to do so.
   - If low stroke volume or depressed LV function is present, and no mechanical obstruction is obvious, observe 48–72 h for hemodynamic recovery; repeat TTE. Immediate reintervention may be premature.

3. Early follow‑up (1 month)
   - Repeat TTE. If gradients fall substantially (as in this case to ~27 mm Hg) and patient is clinically improved, conservative management with surveillance is reasonable.
   - Persistent severe gradients with consistent low indexed AVA and DVI suggest true severe PPM or fixed underexpansion → consider reintervention strategies (post‑dilation, valve fracture if feasible, or surgery—tailored to anatomy and risk).

## Special considerations in layered (TAV‑in‑TAV‑in‑TAV) anatomy
- Radial resistance is higher; full expansion may require higher‑pressure balloons; but higher pressure increases risk of annular/root injury and coronary compromise.
- Commissural alignment and valve selection matter for maximizing effective orifice area and future coronary access.
- CT planning is essential before any aggressive post‑dilation: assess valve‑to‑coronary distance and sinus dimensions. If balloon fracture or aggressive dilation is considered, know whether the inner valve frame is amenable to fracture (some platforms are not).
- In cases with prior endocarditis, ensure infection is completely resolved before reintervention.

## VARC‑3 endpoints and decision thresholds
Use VARC‑3 definitions to judge procedural success and guide next steps:
- Procedural success includes acceptable gradient and no major complications. An immediate mean gradient >40 mm Hg or indexed AVA consistent with severe PPM suggests incomplete success.
- However, VARC‑3 recognizes that hemodynamics can evolve; early device success (within 72 hours) and final device success (up to 30 days) are both relevant—so do not label the case failed based only on immediate echo if the patient is clinically improving and imaging suggests reversible causes.
- Reintervention is indicated when there is true valve dysfunction meeting VARC criteria (symptomatic severe stenosis/regurgitation, haemodynamic compromise) after careful exclusion of reversible contributors.

## Clinical pearls
- Don’t panic on the table solely because Doppler gradients are high—synthesize DVI, indexed AVA, stroke volume index, invasive gradients, and CT anatomy.
- DVI is your friend in layered frames: less influenced by LVOT sizing errors and helpful when LVOT measurement is complicated by prior stents.
- If immediate gradients are high but stroke volume is low or LV is recovering, observe 48–72 h and repeat imaging—many patients improve.
- If you plan post‑dilation in a multi‑stent root, pre‑procedural CT review must confirm coronary safety; consider prophylactic coronary protection if distances are borderline.
- Use VARC‑3 windows: early haemodynamic derangement may not equate to procedural failure. Reassess at 24–72 h and 30 days before radical steps unless the patient is unstable.

## Take‑home messages
- High immediate post‑TAVR gradients can reflect underexpansion, low‑flow physiology, Doppler artefact, or true PPM—distinguish using DVI, indexed AVA, stroke volume index, and temporal trends.
- In layered TAV-in-TAV anatomies, CT-guided planning dictates whether safe post‑dilatation is an option; if unsafe, short-term observation is reasonable when clinical status allows.
- Use early repeat imaging (24–72 h) and a 1‑month reassessment—many gradients improve as LV function and valve expansion evolve; management should be individualized and guided by VARC‑3 success criteria.
- When in doubt, a multidisciplinary heart team discussion that integrates echo, CT, invasive hemodynamics and the patient’s clinical course will yield the safest plan.

This patient’s course—immediate mean gradient 67 mm Hg falling to 27 mm Hg at 1 month with clinical recovery—illustrates the nuance: scary echoes deserve rigorous assessment, not reflexive escalation.



# Fever After TAVR: Don’t Miss Enterococcal PVE

What makes this case interesting? A man in his late 70s with two prior TAVI implants (Lotus → Evolut) developed Enterococcus faecalis prosthetic valve endocarditis (PVE) after a TAV-in-TAV and, once treated, later required a third transcatheter valve (Acurate neo2) — a true TAV-in-TAV-in-TAV. The team used careful CT- and TEE-guided planning, deferred surgery because of prohibitive risk, completed a full course of targeted antibiotics, ensured no residual infection on imaging and blood cultures, and went on to a successful CT-planned reintervention with good 1‑year outcomes. This is a neat, practical lesson-set for anyone seeing fever after TAVR.

## Why Enterococcus matters after TAVR
- Enterococcus faecalis is a common culprit in late PVE and can be indolent: low‑grade fevers, constitutional symptoms, or heart failure may be the only clues.
- Prosthetic material (stent frames, sewing rings, valve leaflets) + prior instrumentation = fertile ground for enterococcal seeding.
- Enterococci can adhere and damage leaflets leading to restenosis or regurgitation that mimics structural valve degeneration.

## Red flags at the bedside
Think PVE in any patient with a transcatheter aortic valve who presents with:
- Persistent fever without clear source, especially >48–72 hours or relapsing episodes.
- New/worsening heart failure symptoms (dyspnoea, fatigue), new murmur, or unexplained decline in LVEF.
- Recurrent bacteremia with enteric organisms (Enterococcus spp.), especially after urogenital/GI procedures or bleeding episodes.
- New conduction disturbances (eg, AV block) or embolic phenomena.
In the reported case the patient had fever, worsening heart failure and positive blood cultures for E. faecalis — classic warning signs.

## Culture strategy — get it right up front
- Obtain multiple sets (3 if possible) of blood cultures from separate venipuncture sites before starting antibiotics. Enterococcus can be transient; repeated sampling improves sensitivity.
- If cultures are positive, save isolates for susceptibility testing and consider PCR/16S if cultures are negative but suspicion remains.
- Repeat blood cultures during therapy to document clearance. Persistent bacteremia mandates re-evaluation for uncontrolled focus (vegetation, abscess, paravalvular leak).

## Imaging: TTE, TEE and MSCT — complementary roles
- TTE is first line but has limited sensitivity for prosthetic valves and layered frames.
- TEE is essential for prosthetic valve endocarditis — higher sensitivity for vegetations, leaflet perforation, paravalvular abscess. In the case, TEE helped exclude active endocarditis before reintervention.
- Multislice CT (MSCT) excels at:
  - Assessing frame integration, stent interactions and landing zones when planning redo TAVI.
  - Detecting paravalvular extension, root/annular abscess and relationship to coronaries.
  - Measuring valve-to-coronary distances and predicting coronary obstruction risk for valve‑in‑valve scenarios.
- Use imaging serially: at diagnosis to confirm/exclude active infection, and after therapy to document resolution before elective reintervention.

## Antibiotic pathway for Enterococcus faecalis PVE
- Enterococcus faecalis PVE requires prolonged, targeted therapy — standard regimens differ from native valve disease.
- Current practice (as reflected in the case): prolonged IV therapy guided by ID specialists (commonly 6 weeks of ampicillin-based therapy for susceptible isolates). Alternatives include ampicillin + ceftriaxone for aminoglycoside-sparing synergy when gentamicin unacceptable.
- Tailor therapy to susceptibility (ampicillin-resistant, VRE change the approach), patient renal function and tolerability.
- Long‑term suppressive therapy is not routine unless eradication is in doubt or prosthetic material cannot be removed — in the case there was no chronic suppressive therapy once infection was cleared.

## When is reintervention (including TAVI) safe after treated PVE?
There’s no one-size-fits-all timing; decisions combine infection control, imaging, valve dysfunction severity and surgical risk.
Key practical checkpoints before elective reintervention:
- Completed recommended full course of appropriate antibiotics.
- Clinical improvement with no fever or signs of ongoing infection.
- Serial negative blood cultures.
- TEE/CT showing no vegetations, no paravalvular extension or abscess.
- Multidisciplinary Heart Team consensus involving interventional cardiology, cardiac surgery, infectious diseases and imaging.
In this case the team waited until after therapy, had negative cultures and clear imaging, and chose TAV-in-TAV-in-TAV because surgical risk was prohibitive.

## Procedural planning pearls for redo TAV (especially TAV-in-TAV and beyond)
- Assume layered stent frames change anatomy: smaller effective lumen, potential for coronary obstruction, and tight landing zones.
- Preprocedural MSCT is indispensable: measure residual lumen, valve-to-coronary distances, sinus dimensions, and model leaflet/neo‑skirt positions.
- Select valve type and intended implantation depth to preserve coronary flow and maximise effective orifice area (supra‑annular valves can help haemodynamics in tight anatomies).
- Aim for commissural alignment where possible — preserves coronary access for future interventions.
- Consider coronary protection strategies (wiring/balloon) if CT shows borderline distances — but if CT indicates safe clearance and the team is confident, it may be omitted as in this case.
- Balloon pre- and post-dilation can expand prior frames and improve leaflet motion; intentional valve fracture is another tool but may be limited in some valve types and in multiple layered frames.
- Plan for the worst: ensure surgical backup availability and clear contingency plans.

## Common pitfalls that delay diagnosis or push premature procedures
- Attributing low‑grade fevers and malaise to COPD, pulmonary causes, or other comorbidities without considering PVE.
- Starting antibiotics before drawing adequate blood cultures — reduces diagnostic yield and hides bacteremia.
- Relying solely on TTE in prosthetic patients — underestimates vegetations.
- Proceeding to reintervention based on haemodynamics alone without confirming eradication of infection on serial cultures and imaging.
- Underestimating the cumulative impact of multiple stent frames on coronary access and future treatment options.

## Take-home messages
- Always consider PVE in any TAVR patient with fever, new heart failure, or bacteremia — Enterococcus faecalis is a classic culprit.
- Draw multiple blood cultures before antibiotics. Repeat cultures to document clearance.
- Use TEE + MSCT: TEE for infection, MSCT for anatomy and reintervention feasibility. Both are required for safe planning.
- Treat enterococcal PVE with prolonged, targeted IV therapy under ID guidance; do not rush reintervention until infection is controlled clinically, microbiologically and on imaging.
- Preprocedural CT-guided planning, commissural alignment and valve selection are critical for redo TAV, especially when multiple frames are present.
- Multidisciplinary decision-making is essential — the reported case shows that with careful infection control and imaging-guided strategy, even a TAV-in-TAV-in-TAV can be feasible and durable at 1 year.

This patient’s course is a good reminder: fever after TAVR is not benign. Early recognition, disciplined culture practice, paired TEE/MSCT assessment and team-based timing decisions can make the difference between failed therapy and a durable, well-planned reintervention.



## Lifetime Valve Strategy in 2025: Making Today’s TAVR Compatible With Tomorrow’s Needs

We used to think of TAVR as a one-and-done therapy for elderly, high-risk patients. The landscape has changed — younger patients, longer survival, and rising numbers of valve-in-valve (ViV) procedures mean you must plan the first TAVR with the next one (and the one after that) in mind. A recent case of TAV-in-TAV-in-TAV after treated prosthetic valve endocarditis (Lotus → Evolut → Acurate neo2) nicely illustrates both what can go right with meticulous planning and what you risk leaving yourself with if you don’t plan ahead.

Why this case is instructive
- The team faced a triple-stented aortic root with prior endocarditis, prohibitive surgical risk, and limited annular area — a worst-case “lifetime” scenario.
- CT-guided planning, commissural alignment and a deliberate choice of a low-profile, supra‑annular valve (Acurate neo2) allowed a successful third TAVR with preserved coronary flow and sustained 1‑year function.
- The case highlights the trade-offs every operator must consider at index TAVR: immediate haemodynamics vs preserving future options.

What to think about at your index TAVR (and why it matters)
- Coronary preservation is everything. When you implant a THV, you’re defining future valve-to-coronary (VTC) geometry and the cumulative mesh that can “jail” coronaries in subsequent procedures. Low VTC and high frame height limit future reaccess and increase risk of coronary obstruction on repeat ViV.
- Commissural alignment now matters. Rotational alignment of the THV relative to native commissures reduces overlap of valve frame posts with coronary ostia and eases future coronary access and commissural alignment for subsequent THVs.
- Choose frame and leaflet position with an eye to future EOA. Supra‑annular leaflets yield better EOA in small annuli and narrow ViV landing zones — important to minimize prosthesis–patient mismatch (PPM) down the road.
- Keep cumulative frame height low. Taller frames stacked create more metal mesh and reduce effective orifice area; consider lower-profile frames when anatomy allows.
- Preserve the landing zone. Implant depth at the index TAVR can influence how much leaflet tissue or native calcium will be pushed outward/inward on future ViV deployments. A high, controlled implantation may help preserve coronary clearance later.

Practical pre‑procedural planning — imaging and CT work-up
- Do MSCT in ViV planning mode from day one. Measure sinus of Valsalva, coronary heights, VTC (projected distance after anticipated valve implantation), sinotubular junction, and valve frame-to-coronary relationships.
- Model future scenarios. If possible, run virtual valve overlay and simulate post‑implantation VTC and neo-skirt position. That’s what guided the Acurate neo2 implantation in the triple‑TAVR case.
- Ask: will the next valve create a neo-skirt at coronary level? If yes, consider strategies to avoid it at the index implant.

Choice of valve at index — key considerations
- Supra‑annular vs intra‑annular: in small annuli or younger patients where future ViV is likely, supra‑annular valves often give better haemodynamics and lower PPM risk on reinterventions.
- Frame height and cell architecture: low frame height and larger open cells ease coronary access. Balloon-expandable frames have different re-expansion and fracture characteristics compared with self‑expanding frames — choose based on both index haemodynamics and future plan.
- Commissural alignment capability: some devices offer markers or implantation techniques that facilitate predictable orientation — use them.

Strategies to mitigate PPM across the lifetime
- Use supra‑annular valves where appropriate.
- Consider initial slight oversizing or high implant positioning to preserve leaflet opening — but balance against risk of paravalvular leak or annular injury.
- Plan for bioprosthetic valve fracture (BVF) at the time of ViV when using balloon-expandable surgical bioprostheses or some THV frames (note: not universally feasible). BVF can increase EOA substantially in certain constructs, but it requires the right valve type and careful preplanning.
- When stented frames are already multiple, the haemodynamic gains from BVF may be limited or unsafe — assess case-by-case.

Where BASILICA and BVF fit — and where they don’t
- BASILICA (intentional leaflet laceration) is a powerful tool to prevent coronary obstruction by splitting native or bioprosthetic leaflets that would otherwise be pinned up during ViV. It’s best used when you have an intact leaflet that will form a neo‑skirt threatening the coronary ostium.
- BASILICA limitations: its efficacy is unproven in heavily layered frames (multiple THVs) where the skirt is a metal mesh rather than native leaflets; traversing and lacerating layers becomes nearly impossible. In triple-stent anatomies, BASILICA often won’t help.
- BVF can be very useful for surgical bioprostheses or some THVs where ring fracture is possible to boost EOA. But BVF requires that the outer frame can be fractured without catastrophic annular injury or coronary compromise. In multiple THV frames, fracture might not transmit effectively to inner frames or might have unpredictable effects on coronary clearance.
- Bottom line: both BASILICA and BVF are adjuncts, not panaceas. Their utility depends on device type, anatomy, and the number/layering of existing frames.

Procedural tactics that helped in the TAV‑in‑TAV‑in‑TAV case (and you can borrow)
- CT-guided selection of a low‑height, supra‑annular valve (Acurate neo2) to maximize residual EOA.
- Aim for commissural alignment to preserve coronary access.
- High implantation relative to prior leaflets to prevent inward leaflet folding and avoid creating a neo-skirt under the coronaries.
- Pre‑ and post‑dilation strategically used to optimize expansion of prior frames (post‑dilation can partially expand a self-expanding frame like Evolut and improve orifice area).
- Meticulous exclusion of active infection before reintervention — prior endocarditis is not an absolute contraindication once treated, but it changes risk calculus.

When to favor surgery instead of serial TAVR
- Active uncontrolled prosthetic infection, annular/root abscess, or complex paravalvular destruction usually mandates surgery.
- If serial ViV (or ViV-in-ViV) would produce unacceptably small EOA with high PPM risk and the patient is a reasonable surgical candidate, root replacement might be the better durable solution.
- In very young patients, consider lifetime strategy and counsel on the likelihood of future surgical reintervention.

Takeaways — practical clinical pearls
- Think lifetime, not just index: choose a device and implant strategy that balance immediate hemodynamics with future coronary access and reintervention options.
- Use pre-procedural CT and virtual valve simulation to estimate VTC, neo-skirt formation, and cumulative frame effects.
- Favor commissural alignment and consider supra‑annular valves in small annuli to reduce future PPM.
- Realize the limits of rescue tools: BASILICA is less helpful in multilayered stent anatomies; BVF only works with susceptible valve constructs.
- When infection has been treated and excluded by imaging and cultures, ViV (even repeat ViV) can be feasible — but decisions must be individualized and multidisciplinary.
- Document your implant orientation and depth clearly — that record may be lifesaving for the next operator.

Final thought
Every TAVR implant writes the next chapter of that patient’s valve story. If you want options later, you must preserve them now — through device selection, commissural alignment, CT-driven planning, and a clear discussion with the patient about lifetime strategy. The triple‑TAVR case shows it can be done — but it takes forethought, imaging, and a team willing to plan for the next chapter before closing the cover on the first.

Key take-home messages
- Plan index TAVR with future ViV in mind: commissural alignment, VTC preservation, low frame height, and supra‑annular leaflet choice matter.
- Use MSCT and virtual valve overlays to predict neo-skirt and coronary clearance.
- BASILICA and BVF are useful tools but have limits in multi-layered THV anatomies.
- In treated endocarditis without active infection, re‑TAVR can be considered when surgical risk is prohibitive — multidisciplinary decision-making is essential.
- Document valve orientation and implantation details to aid future interventions.



# Sizing the Unsizable: Measuring True Inner Lumen for ViV-in-ViV

What makes this case worth a close look is obvious: a TAV‑in‑TAV‑in‑TAV in a frail, comorbid septuagenarian after treated prosthetic valve endocarditis — and a great 1‑year outcome. The technical secret sauce wasn’t luck; it was CT‑guided humility. When you already have one metallic scaffolding in the root — let alone two — the job isn’t “measure annulus and pick size.” It’s about mapping the remaining true inner lumen, understanding how frames and leaflets interact, and tailoring dilation and device choice to preserve coronary flow and optimize residual effective orifice area (EOA) without blowing up the root.

Below are the imaging nuances that matter, illustrated by this patient’s journey (Lotus → Evolut TAV‑in‑TAV → Acurate neo2 ViV‑in‑ViV). Think of this as a checklist for sizing the unsizable.

## What CT must answer in layered‑frame anatomy

1. Minimum true inner lumen (TIL)
- The single most important metric. Measure the smallest lumen within the inner-most frame at the intended landing zone (often the level of prior valve leaflets). Use multiplanar reconstructions and centerline perpendicular sections. In the case above the Evolut TIL was 13.7 mm — the number that drove the whole plan.
- Don’t rely on labelled “label” valve sizes or nominal internal diameters — the stacked frames and leaflet tissue will reduce the functional lumen.

2. 3D shape and ellipticity
- Measure orthogonal diameters and report an eccentricity index (max/min). Elliptical lumens alter contact pressures and balloon behavior; a nominal diameter may be misleading.
- High ellipticity predicts asymmetric expansion, focal high stresses, and potential for underexpansion or root injury if oversized balloons are used.

3. Leaflet status and calcification
- Are prior valve leaflets fibrosed, calcified, fused, or mobile? Infected/treated leaflets can be stiff and folded inward, causing obstruction. Calcified or stiff leaflets limit frame expansion and increase risk of leaflet “napkin‑ring” obstruction.
- CT can also show leaflet bulk that will form the neo‑skirt after your implant — critical for coronary obstruction risk.

4. Frame‑to‑frame relationships
- Map the vertical and radial position of each frame, stent heights, and overlap zones. Identify where frames buttress each other and where there are gaps.
- Note frame material and radial strength (e.g., supra‑annular self‑expanding Evolut vs low‑profile frames). This informs whether balloon post‑dilation will meaningfully expand the prior frame or only risk scaffold fracture/root injury.

5. Valve‑to‑coronary distance and sinus anatomy
- Standard valve‑to‑coronary (VTC) and sinus of Valsalva measurements still apply, but now consider the height of a neo‑skirt created by two layers of leaflets plus your new implant.
- Even if nominal VTC looks acceptable, cumulative skirt height and commissural misalignment can turn an acceptable anatomy into one that requires coronary protection or BASILICA planning.

6. Landing zone selection
- Decide whether a higher (leaflet‑level) implant to tether old leaflets against the frame is preferable versus a deeper implant. The case above used high implantation to fix prior leaflets outward and minimize neo‑skirt, improving EOA.

## Translating measurements into procedural strategy

1. Device choice: match design to the constrained lumen
- Supra‑annular designs with low frame height (e.g., Acurate neo2) can preserve EOA in tight anatomies. If the TIL is very small, consider valves known for better hemodynamics at small ID.
- Avoid tall, bulky frames that will further encroach on sinuses or hinder coronary access unless CT supports it.

2. Balloon sizing for pre‑ and post‑dilation
- Balloon diameter should be chosen against TIL, not nominal surgical annulus. In the presence of ellipticity, choose a balloon that respects the smaller diameter; oversizing risks aortic root injury or stent distortion.
- If you anticipate expanding a chronically embedded self‑expanding frame (Evolut, CoreValve family), high‑pressure post‑dilation can partially increase lumen and leaflet motion — but expect resistance. Know the limits: attempting to “force” a rigid, layered anatomy can lead to aortic injury.
- Consider gradual step‑wise dilation (undersized → nominal) with hemodynamic assessment between inflations.

3. Pre‑dilation strategy
- Pre‑dilation helps assess how the old leaflets and frames will behave. In this case, a 22 mm balloon was used for predilation under rapid pacing to test coronary flow and leaflet movement.
- If pre‑dilation shows coronary compromise or excessive leaflet prolapse, be ready with coronary protection or bail‑out plans.

4. Coronary protection and commissural alignment
- CT should guide whether you need upfront coronary protection (wires, undeployed stents) or a BASILICA strategy. Absence of a low VTC doesn’t mean no risk — cumulative skirt and unpredictable leaflet folding can obstruct coronaries.
- Try to plan commissural alignment with fluoroscopic landmarks from CT: aim to orient the new frame to avoid overlapping coronary columns; it preserves future access and reduces obstruction risk.

5. Implantation depth and the neo‑skirt
- High implant of the third valve in front of the Evolut leaflets can tack the leaflets open and minimize the neo‑skirt under the coronaries — improving EOA and coronary safety.
- But high implants can increase gradient if you seat leaflets too supra‑annularly or if sealing is suboptimal. Balance hemodynamics and coronary preservation.

6. Expect and plan for prosthesis‑patient mismatch (PPM)
- Triple frames have limited ceiling for EOA. Even with perfect technique, immediate post‑implant gradients may be high (as in this case: mean gradient 67 mm Hg immediately post‑op then improved over weeks to 26–27 mm Hg).
- Communicate realistic expectations: early high gradients may decrease as frames settle and prior self‑expanding scaffolds allow partial recoil.

## Avoiding root injury — practical cautions

- Respect the TIL and ellipticity. Don’t use balloon diameters that exceed the smallest inner dimension without a compelling reason.
- High‑pressure or large balloon bursts in a layered metallic root are a potential recipe for aortic rupture or stent fracture.
- If you plan aggressive expansion, have surgical backup or covered stent options ready.
- In infected prostheses that are treated and sterile, tissue planes may still be friable; avoid traumatic maneuvers.

## Imaging workflow recommendations

- Start with thin‑slice gated CT with centerline reconstructions. Measure TIL at multiple short‑axis levels (annulus, leaflet tips, mid‑frame).
- Generate 3D volume renderings and virtual valve implantation to estimate neo‑skirt and VTC; dedicated ViV software adds value where available.
- Correlate with TEE/fluoroscopy for leaflet mobility and to confirm no active infection.
- Share CT maps with the entire heart team: interventionalist, imager, surgeon, and infectious disease.

## Clinical pearl: size the lumen, not the label
The single most common misstep in ViV‑in‑ViV planning is relying on valve labels or surgical annulus sizing. In layered frames, the functional constraint is the minimal true inner lumen and its shape. Let that number drive your balloon choice, device selection, and implantation depth.

## Take‑home points
- Measure the minimum true inner lumen (TIL) inside the innermost frame — that’s the critical sizing metric in ViV‑in‑ViV.
- Report ellipticity, leaflet bulk/calcification, frame‑to‑frame overlap zones, and valve‑to‑coronary distances; these change strategy.
- Choose valve type and size to maximize EOA in the constrained lumen (supra‑annular, low‑frame options often preferred).
- Balloon sizing must be based on TIL and the eccentricity; avoid aggressive oversizing that risks root injury.
- Pre‑dilation is a test — be ready with coronary protection or BASILICA if the neo‑skirt threatens coronaries.
- Plan commissural alignment and a high implant when appropriate to tack old leaflets outward, reduce neo‑skirt, and preserve coronary access.
- Expect early high gradients; documented partial expansion of prior frames and post‑dilation can improve hemodynamics over weeks.
- Multidisciplinary CT‑led planning is non‑negotiable for complex ViV‑in‑ViV cases.

This patient’s success hinged on meticulous CT workup, conservative and pragmatic balloon strategy, device selection that favored supra‑annular EOA, and deliberate high implantation to manage the neo‑skirt. When the anatomy looks “unsizable,” it usually isn’t unsizable — it’s under‑measured. Measure smarter, not bigger.



# Vascular Access After a Pseudoaneurysm: Reducing Complications in Redo TAVR

What jumps out from this case isn’t just the technical feat of a TAV-in-TAV-in-TAV after treated infective endocarditis — it’s that the same groin that once bled and required open repair was later used successfully for another large-bore TAVR. That prior pulsatile femoral hematoma and pseudoaneurysm (post–TAV-in-TAV in 2022) could easily have derailed any attempt at repeat transfemoral access. Instead, careful preprocedural planning, targeted imaging and an evidence-based access strategy helped the team reuse the femoral route in 2024 without vascular complications.

If you’re planning redo large-bore access in a patient with prior femoral injury, surgical repair or chronic scarring, here are the pragmatic lessons — distilled from this case and from practical vascular-access best practices — that should be part of every heart-team checklist.

## Start with anatomy: iliofemoral CT mapping is non-negotiable
This patient had MSCT as part of valve planning, and that same scan provides the roadmap for vascular access. For redo or scarred groins you must know:

- Luminal diameter along the iliofemoral tract (minimums for sheath sizes).
- Calcification distribution (concentric vs eccentric) and femoral plaque that increases rupture risk.
- Vessel tortuosity that can make sheath passage or closure device apposition difficult.
- Prior repair material (stitches, patches) and residual pseudoaneurysm cavity or false lumen.
- Distance from skin to femoral artery and depth of artery — affects ultrasound visualization and closure-device choice.

MSCT lets you pick the optimal side and puncture level (common femoral artery vs external iliac), anticipate difficulties, and decide in advance whether surgical exposure or an alternate route is safer.

## Use ultrasound every time — especially in scarred groins
Ultrasound-guided puncture is standard of care for large-bore access. In the setting of prior hematoma/pseudoaneurysm, ultrasound helps you:

- Avoid scar tissue and hematoma cavities.
- Identify the true common femoral artery and the bifurcation.
- Choose the most favorable puncture site where artery is soft, non-calcified, and compressible.
- Guide micropuncture technique to reduce the chance of failure or posterior-wall puncture.

Take your time. If you can’t get a safe window, abort and consider an alternative plan.

## Think contralateral angiographic support and safety wires
When re-accessing a previously injured femoral, have a bailout strategy wired and ready:

- Contralateral femoral or radial secondary access for roadmapping and angiography.
- Place a contralateral crossover wire and an angioplasty balloon parked in the ipsilateral external iliac/common femoral artery before upsizing — ready to tamponade bleeding.
- A safety covered stent should be on the shelf (or the cath lab team briefed and primed) for catastrophic vessel injury.

In this case the team used left radial and left femoral arterial access — having radial adjuncts helps with angiography and reduces risk.

## Plan puncture level and closure device selection up front
Prior pseudoaneurysm or open repair changes how closure devices behave. Choices to consider:

- Suture-based devices (ProGlide) are preferred for their track record in controlled large-bore closures. However, in scarred tissue their footplate may sit improperly and failure rates increase.
- Collagen plugs (e.g., Angio-Seal) are less suitable alone for very large sheaths but can be used as adjuncts in hybrid strategies.
- In heavily scarred tissue or prior failed percutaneous closure, surgical cutdown may be safest.
- Consider a preclosure technique (two ProGlide sutures) if vessel anatomy allows — but be ready to supplement with balloon tamponade or surgical repair if sutures don’t seal.

Pre-procedural imaging helps you choose the device: shallow vessel vs deep vessel, calcified anterior wall, or prior groin surgery all push toward surgical exposure.

## Consider alternate access early — don’t stick with the groin for pride
If CT or ultrasound suggests unsafe transfemoral access because of severe scarring, small lumen, heavy calcification or a prior complicated repair, move to an alternate access rather than forcing the femoral route:

- Transaxillary/subclavian (surgical cutdown or percutaneous): good option if vessels are suitable.
- Transcarotid: increasing in use with favorable outcomes in selected patients.
- Direct aortic (surgical mini-sternotomy or mini-thoracotomy): considers higher surgical risk but sometimes the only option.
- Femoral surgical cutdown with vascular surgeon standby: hybrid solution when transfemoral is preferable but percutaneous closure is judged risky.

Shared decision-making with cardiac surgery and vascular surgery — early in planning — keeps options realistic and safe.

## Procedural tactics that reduce vascular complication risk
Small steps in technique lower the odds of catastrophe:

- Start with micropuncture and upsizing over a wire; avoid “stabbing” with a large-bore needle in scarred tissue.
- Use fluoroscopic and ultrasound landmarks together for precise anterior-wall puncture.
- Keep sheath dwell time minimal and exchange wires only when stable.
- Consider balloon occlusion from contralateral side for controlled hemostasis during sheath removal or suture tying.
- If performing preclosure, cinch sutures sequentially and check leak under angiography before removing the sheath.
- Have a plan for immediate conversion to open repair and the right surgical instruments available.

## Postprocedural surveillance and early intervention
Patients with prior pseudoaneurysm deserve early and deliberate post-op vascular checks:

- Immediate angiography after closure to assess for extravasation or flow-limiting dissection.
- Early duplex ultrasound (within 24 hours) to look for a new pseudoaneurysm or hematoma.
- Low threshold to re-image or consult vascular surgery if any groin pain, expanding hematoma or drop in hemoglobin is seen.

In the presented case, the team avoided repeat vascular complications by combining preprocedural CT, appropriate imaging-guided puncture and a cautious procedural approach — and surveillance confirmed an uncomplicated recovery.

## Practical checklist for redo large-bore access after a prior pseudoaneurysm

- Preprocedural:
  - Review prior vascular complication details and any operative notes.
  - Obtain dedicated iliofemoral CT angiography for vessel diameter, calcification, scarring, and prior repair assessment.
  - Multidisciplinary discussion (interventional cardiology, cardiac surgery, vascular surgery, imaging).
  - Decide preferred access side and backup access/route.

- In the lab:
  - Ultrasound-guided micropuncture technique for all femoral access.
  - Contralateral/radial access for angiographic mapping and safety wire.
  - Park an internal iliac/contralateral balloon and/or covered stent on standby when appropriate.
  - Use preclosure with suture-based devices when anatomy permits; be ready with adjunct balloon tamponade or surgical backup.
  - Keep closure strategy documented and rehearsed (who will tie sutures, who will inflate balloon, etc).

- If anatomy unfavourable:
  - Move early to alternate access (axillary, carotid, direct aortic) or surgical cutdown; do not force a risky transfemoral approach.

- Postprocedure:
  - Immediate angiographic check of closure site.
  - Early bedside duplex ultrasound (within 24 hours).
  - Low threshold for vascular surgery consultation for any concern.

Takeaway: a prior femoral pseudoaneurysm is not an absolute contraindication to future transfemoral TAVR, but it raises the stakes. Detailed CT-based vascular mapping, ultrasound-guided puncture, contralateral/radial adjuncts and clear bailout plans (balloons, covered stents, surgical cutdown) are essential. When in doubt, choose the safest access — the patient will thank you for it later.



# Antithrombotics After TAVR With Recurrent GI Bleeding and CAD: Finding the Sweet Spot

What makes this case interesting? A man in his late 70s with multivessel CAD and multiple prior PCIs, a pacemaker, recurrent gastrointestinal bleeding since 2022, and — critically — multiple transcatheter aortic valves (TAV-in-TAV-in-TAV) after treated prosthetic valve endocarditis. He needs a balanced antithrombotic strategy that protects coronary stents and the new transcatheter valve while minimising bleeding and not complicating infective endocarditis follow-up. That tension — ischaemic protection versus catastrophic bleeding — is common in everyday TAVR care, but it becomes acute in patients like this.

Below I pull together practical, evidence-informed guidance you can use at the bedside when faced with TAVR patients who have high bleeding risk and prior PCI.

Why re-think routine DAPT after TAVR?
- Randomised trials and meta-analyses over the past decade have moved the field away from routine dual antiplatelet therapy (DAPT) after TAVR for most patients. The POPular-TAVI (cohort A) trial demonstrated that single antiplatelet therapy (SAPT) reduced bleeding without increasing thrombotic events compared with aspirin plus clopidogrel in patients without an indication for oral anticoagulation. Anticoagulation strategies that add antiplatelets increase bleeding risk; rivaroxaban-based strategies (eg, GALILEO) raised safety concerns. In short: bleeding harm from more antithrombotic therapy often outweighs modest and uncertain ischemic benefit after TAVR in most patients.
- The calculus flips only when there is a recent coronary stent (<1 month, sometimes <6 months depending on stent type and clinical scenario), high thrombotic risk, or another compelling indication for antiplatelet combination.

Applying this to our case
- This patient’s coronary stents were implanted years ago (2009–2014). Late stent thrombosis risk after modern DES beyond the first year is low. There is no indication from the case timeline that he had recent PCI that would mandate DAPT.
- He has a high bleeding burden (recurrent GI bleeding since 2022). That pushes strongly toward minimising antithrombotic intensity.
- No chronic atrial fibrillation or other ongoing indication for oral anticoagulation is described; if present, anticoagulation strategy would change (see below).
- Prior prosthetic valve endocarditis was treated and resolved; no ongoing infection or suppressive antibiotic therapy was in place. That means standard antithrombotic considerations apply (no extra antiplatelet role for PVE).

Practical antithrombotic strategy — a framework
1. Assess indications for antiplatelet or anticoagulant therapy independently:
   - Recent PCI (<1–6 months): consider cardiology consensus — usually continue DAPT for the minimal effective duration then stop to SAPT.
   - Longstanding stents (>1 year) without recent events: SAPT only is reasonable.
   - Atrial fibrillation or other indications for chronic oral anticoagulation: DOAC/VKA alone is generally preferred after TAVR; avoid routine addition of antiplatelet unless recent PCI mandates short overlap.
2. Default for high-bleeding-risk TAVR patients without other indications: single antiplatelet therapy (usually low-dose aspirin) rather than DAPT.
3. If already on single antiplatelet but significant GI bleeding risk exists: discuss switching to clopidogrel vs aspirin based on bleeding profile, or consider stopping antiplatelet therapy altogether after shared decision-making — some very high bleeding‑risk patients may be managed without antiplatelet therapy if coronary stents are remote and there is no other indication.
4. If oral anticoagulation is required:
   - Use DOAC or VKA alone post‑TAVR; avoid routine addition of antiplatelet drugs.
   - If recent DES: use the shortest possible period of combined therapy (eg, DOAC + single antiplatelet for 1 month), then step down to DOAC alone, balancing bleeding risk.
5. Always personalise duration: the “one-size-fits-all” DAPT prescriptions after PCI do not apply post-TAVR.

Coordination with GI and use of protective strategies
- Partner with gastroenterology early to identify and treat bleeding sources (eg, angiodysplasia, peptic disease, malformations) before or after TAVR when possible.
- Start proton pump inhibitor (PPI) therapy when antiplatelet therapy is given for upper GI protection, after assessing risks and interactions.
- Consider endoscopic therapy or interventional radiology for recurrent or ongoing bleeding sources; in some cases stabilising the GI bleed allows safer antithrombotic management.
- If bleeding is active or uncontrolled, prioritise haemorrhage control and minimise antithrombotics; re-evaluate coronary and valve thrombosis risk after stabilisation.

Endocarditis considerations — what to tell the patient and team
- Prior treated prosthetic valve endocarditis does not, by itself, mandate a special long-term antithrombotic regimen. What matters is that infection is fully treated and no suppressive antibiotics are ongoing.
- Continue standard infective endocarditis prevention measures per current guidelines: emphasise dental hygiene, prompt treatment of bacteraemia sources, and selective antibiotic prophylaxis for high‑risk dental procedures (valve prosthesis is a recognized high‑risk condition for which dental prophylaxis is recommended).
- Any new febrile illness, persistent bacteremia, or change in valve function should trigger urgent evaluation (blood cultures, TEE, CT) given the patient’s prosthetic history.

Monitoring and follow-up
- Close follow-up after TAVR in high-bleeding-risk patients: early clinical checks for bleeding or ischaemia at 1 month, and echocardiography to assess valve function as clinically indicated.
- Reassess the need for ongoing antithrombotic therapy at defined intervals (eg, 1, 3, and 6 months), and after any bleeding or ischemic event.
- If antiplatelet therapy is continued, educate the patient about bleeding signs, medication interactions, and the need for PPI if upper GI risk is present.

Clinical pearls
- For most TAVR patients without a separate indication for antithrombotic therapy, SAPT is sufficient and safer than routine DAPT.
- In patients with remote (>1 year) coronary stents and a high bleeding risk, SAPT — or even no antiplatelet in extreme bleeding risk after multidisciplinary discussion — is reasonable.
- If chronic anticoagulation (eg, AF) is required, DOAC or VKA alone is usually best; adding antiplatelet increases bleeding without clear benefit except when recent stenting forces short-term combination therapy.
- Coordinate early with GI and interventional teams to treat bleeding sources, start PPI where appropriate, and limit antithrombotic exposure when bleeding risk is high.
- Prior prosthetic valve endocarditis does not necessarily change antithrombotic decisions if infection is resolved; however, it mandates vigilant infection surveillance and standard prophylaxis measures.

Bottom line for this patient
Given his recurrent GI bleeding and remote coronary stents, the balance favours minimising antithrombotic intensity after the third TAVR. Unless he has a concurrent, separate indication for anticoagulation or a recent PCI, single antiplatelet therapy (usually low‑dose aspirin) or even stopping antiplatelet therapy after careful multidisciplinary discussion is defensible. Coordinate with GI to aggressively pursue bleeding source control and use PPI prophylaxis where indicated. Maintain vigilant endocarditis surveillance and standard dental prophylaxis practices.

